 How about in the case of an unexpected emergency? The U.S. Department of Education has released an emergency assistance toolkit to help districts and schools navigate emergency situations.

In May, the department hosted a special virtual event highlighting various efforts to address how school closures will impact children during a crisis.

During the meeting, attendees learned about plans for improving student access to vital supplies like food and water; ensuring continued access to technology and the internet; and providing psychological and emotional support to children in need.

One of the new resources recently released by the Department includes the "Emergency Assistance Toolkit." The toolkit includes a variety of resources aimed at helping schools and districts to cope with the challenges associated with such a crisis. Some of the resources include:

- A sample school district emergency communications plan;
- A resource list for educational planning and emergency management tools;
- An emergency operations planning overview for higher education;
- National standards for family school partnerships in a disaster;
- A toolkit for higher education institutions to address the needs of students impacted by a disaster;
- Resources to address the needs of students with special needs in a disaster; and
- An instructional continuity planning toolkit.

Attendees can also download the Emergency Preparedness Toolkit for Institutions of Higher Education, as well as the Emergency Assistance Webinar: Resources for School-Based Programs & Services During Emergencies

More information is available at: https://r.ed.gov/crises
